; A bare plain group in a table's KEY or VALUE domain. A CBOR map entry holds exactly ONE item in
; each of its two slots, and a keyless group has no single-item form — the only emission available
; is splicing its members in flat, which writes N items where the map header promised one. The
; NAMED spelling used to do exactly that at exit 0 (one entry of `{ * uint => coords }` serialized
; to `a2 01 07 08 02 09 0a`, which any other CBOR decoder reads as the 2-entry map `{1: 7, 8: 2}`
; plus trailing bytes) while the INLINE spelling reached a raw `unwrap` at generation, so both are
; refused here: a graceful refusal replaces a broken acceptance rather than sitting beside it.
; Every spelling of the shape, since they reach the one seam through two consumers:
;   - `a`  named rule, VALUE role.
;   - `b`  named rule, KEY role.
;   - `c`  inline table (the spelling that panicked), VALUE role.
;   - `d`  inline table, KEY role — and both roles at once is `e`.
;   - `f`  parenthesized table (`{ * (k => v) }`), a second arm of the same seam.
;   - `g`  an ALIAS to the group, caught through shallow alias resolution.
; Green neighbours, kept here so a refactor that over-widens the guard shows up as a catalog flip:
; `h` is the remedy the message names — an array-WRAPPED group carries `basic_override`, so it
; serializes as ONE nested item (`{1: [7,8]}` → `a1 01 82 07 08`, conformant, no trailing bytes) —
; and `i` is the array-ELEMENT placement of the same group, which stays supported because an
; array's emitted length scales with the group's arity, so splicing there IS conformant.
; A single-element parenthesized rule (`one = (uint)`) is a transparent alias rather than a group
; and never reaches the guard; a `[…]`/`{…}` rule is a real struct and is likewise untouched.
coords = (uint, uint)
a = { * uint => coords }
b = { * coords => uint }
c = [{ * uint => coords }]
d = [{ * coords => uint }]
e = [{ * coords => coords }]
f = { * (uint => coords) }
g_alias = coords
g = { * uint => g_alias }
h = { * uint => [coords] }
i = [* coords]
